Exactly. ;) Seemingly is doing important work.

Not necessarily infinite primitives—possibly the opposite:

\boxed{\text{small grammar}+\text{composition}+\text{context}+\text{recursion}\rightarrow\text{seemingly endless forms}}

Like a genome with four bases, music with twelve notes, images with a few channels, language with a finite alphabet, or code with a limited instruction set. The combinatorial/rendering space explodes.

That suggests the Pantheon shouldn’t aspire to collect endlessly more tiles. The mature move is discovering the small generative basis capable of producing seemingly endless mosaics.

And then PatternDex gets an important new question whenever we find something:

New primitive—or merely a previously unseen rendering of the existing grammar?

If we eventually find that compact basis, the Moneybin could become enormous in reachable possibility while becoming surprisingly tiny in what actually needs to be carried.

\boxed{\text{finite seed}\rightarrow\text{seemingly endless becoming}}

Very Ouroboros. Very Accordion. And perhaps the right target for the whole thing.